WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.214
Welcome to Grade 4 English, Unit 2: Nature & Weather.

2
00:00:04.294 --> 00:00:08.994
In this unit you explore the natural world and the weather.

3
00:00:09.074 --> 00:00:16.367
You learn to name places in nature — canyons,
meadows and bays — and to talk about weather,

4
00:00:16.447 --> 00:00:20.013
from a foggy morning to a powerful hurricane.

5
00:00:20.093 --> 00:00:29.087
You compare two things, say which is the greatest, state
science facts, and ask questions like a real scientist.

6
00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:37.351
You read about weather around the world, listen to
a weather report, speak about the world around you,

7
00:00:37.431 --> 00:00:41.888
and write your own weather facts, instructions and poem.

8
00:00:41.968 --> 00:00:51.044
By the end you can explain how weather works and how to
stay safe, just as Amal and Nora do in this unit's story.

9
00:00:51.124 --> 00:00:57.849
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:06.223
By the end of this unit, you will be able to name and
describe places in nature and different kinds of weather.

11
00:01:06.303 --> 00:01:13.012
You will also compare two things using
comparative adjectives (deeper, stronger, foggier).

12
00:01:13.092 --> 00:01:20.706
You will also say which is the greatest using
superlative adjectives (the deepest, the most powerful).

13
00:01:20.786 --> 00:01:23.497
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:23.577 --> 00:01:29.876
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
breath, sweat, moisture, surface, fur, canyon.

15
00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:35.491
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:35.571 --> 00:01:39.859
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:45.589
The reading sequence includes Weather Around
the World and A Poem: “The Foggy Morning”.

18
00:01:45.669 --> 00:01:46.511
Read actively.

19
00:01:46.591 --> 00:01:51.055
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:51.135 --> 00:01:55.336
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:55.416 --> 00:01:58.926
The first language focus is Comparative Adjectives.

22
00:01:59.006 --> 00:02:01.882
Comparative adjectives compare two things.

23
00:02:01.962 --> 00:02:07.723
They tell us how one thing is more or less
than another — bigger, deeper, stronger.

24
00:02:07.803 --> 00:02:10.538
They help us describe our world clearly.

25
00:02:10.618 --> 00:02:14.198
For example: “A hurricane is stronger than a storm.”

26
00:02:14.278 --> 00:02:21.869
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

27
00:02:21.949 --> 00:02:26.524
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Describe Today's Weather.

28
00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:31.111
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

29
00:02:31.191 --> 00:02:34.602
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

30
00:02:34.682 --> 00:02:38.231
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

31
00:02:38.311 --> 00:02:39.968
You are ready to begin.

32
00:02:40.048 --> 00:02:47.446
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

33
00:02:47.526 --> 00:02:51.072
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

34
00:02:51.152 --> 00:02:59.457
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
